<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaungsithu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1090–<a href="/years/1167">1167</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Sithu I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alaungsithu, also known as Sithu I, was king of Pagan from about 1112 to 1167 and the grandson and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a>. His long reign was a period of internal peace and prosperity, during which he travelled widely in his realm, is credited with standardising weights and measures, and built the Thatbyinnyu, the tallest temple at Pagan. Tradition holds that he was killed by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathu">Narathu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaungsithu.jpg" alt="Alaungsithu" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Richard Carnac Temple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Min_Sithu_Nat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sithu was born Zeyathura Sithu, by one reckoning on 17 January 1090, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saw-yun">Saw Yun</a>, son of King Saw Lu, and Shwe Einthi, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyansittha">Kyansittha</a>; the Zatadawbon Yazawin gives 13 December 1089, and the chronicles disagree on many dates of his life. Kyansittha, who thought he had no son, crowned the infant and presented him to the people as their king, saying he would reign only as regent. A son of Kyansittha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajakumar">Yazakumar</a>, did exist but made no claim, and Sithu succeeded without opposition, crowned by the aging Primate Shin Arahan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The early part of the reign was spent repressing revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> and northern Arakan. In Arakan a usurper, Kahtom, had driven out the rightful heir; a first combined land and seaborne invasion failed, but a second in 1118 restored Letya Min Nan, who in gratitude repaired the Buddhagaya shrine in Sithu's honour. Sithu travelled widely on pious pilgrimages, reportedly sailing as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and visiting the Nanzhao Kingdom, and there was apparently much disorder during his long absences. The rulings of his court once existed in a collection, the Alaungsithu Hpyatton.</p><p>His standardised weights and measures gave an impetus to the monetisation of Pagan's economy. In the 1150s he visited the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parakramabahu-i">Parakramabahu I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and appointed an ambassador; the Burmese chronicles say he married Parakramabahu's daughter, but the Sri Lankan chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/culavamsa">Cūḷavaṃsa</a> says Pagan captured a Sinhalese princess on her way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, sparking a brief naval war. His reign saw a shift from Mon architecture towards a Burman style: the Thatbyinnyu was consecrated in 1144, and he also built the Shwegugyi Temple next to the palace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the 1160s Sithu banished his eldest son and heir, Min Shin Saw, for ill treatment of people, and made his second son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathu">Narathu</a> heir apparent. When the king fell ill in 1167, Narathu moved him from the palace to the Shwegugyi Temple, and when Sithu regained consciousness, furious at being set aside, Narathu smothered him with bedclothes. For his pious deeds he is remembered as Alaungsithu, Sithu the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya Buddha</a>, and as Min Sithu he is one of the 37 nats of the Burmese pantheon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alaungsithu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Alaungsithu</b>, whose long reign lasted until c. 1167, is credited with a standard system of weights and measures and with the Thatbyinnyu temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Bagan, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1140" aria-label="Bagan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alaungsithu"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5387 5744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5387 5744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.17,94.86&amp;year=1140">What was here in 1140? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
